    <h1>Maemiseong: the castle a shipyard engineer built by hand</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      From Busan, Geoje is only a bridge away. Skip the usual checklist for a moment and start at
      Maemiseong, a free stone wall rising from a quiet fishing village. The photos look like a
      European castle. The story is pure Geoje.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>At a glance</strong><br />
      Address: 29 Bokhang-gil, Jangmok-myeon, Geoje-si, Gyeongsangnam-do<br />
      Hours & parking: free, open 24 hours · public parking nearby<br />
      Stay: 1–1.5 hours · best around sunset<br />
      Tourist info: Geoje Tourist Information +82-55-639-4178
    </div>

    <h2>Why does this place feel so Geoje?</h2>
    <p>
      In September 2003, Typhoon Maemi tore across the south coast. Baek Sun-sam, a ship-design
      researcher from Geoje’s shipyards, lost a seaside field of about 600 pyeong overnight. He was
      not a trained builder. He began stacking stones so the next storm would not take the land again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weekend after weekend, square granite blocks went up and the gaps were filled with cement.
      More than twenty years later the work is still unfinished. The wall is introduced as about
      13 meters at its highest point and more than 100 meters long (Digital Encyclopedia of Geoje
      Culture). People began calling it Maemiseong after the typhoon. Geoje City later listed it
      among the city’s Nine Scenic Views.
    </p>
    <p>
      Geoje builds giant ships from steel. Here, a man who once designed ships built a wall from
      stone. That is why the place feels local rather than generic “Instagram Korea.”
    </p>

    <h2>How do you get there from Busan?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bus:</strong> From Hadan Station take bus 2000, get off at Daekeum Intersection, then walk about 5–10 minutes into the village.</li>
      <li><strong>Car:</strong> Cross Geoga Bridge (toll). Use the public parking lot and avoid driving deep into village alleys.</li>
      <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Weekend afternoon buses back to Busan can fill up. If you are day-tripping from Busan, visit Maemiseong on the way into Geoje.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What should you look for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Arch window photo spot:</strong> Geoga Bridge frames itself through the stone opening. Weekends get busy.</li>
      <li><strong>Watchtower view:</strong> Look toward Geoga Bridge and the Gadukdo side of the sea.</li>
      <li><strong>Cobble beach:</strong> Under the wall, listen to stones rolling with the waves. This is not an official swimming beach.</li>
      <li><strong>Bokhang village lanes:</strong> Cafés appeared, but people still live here. Walk quietly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A gentle overnight route</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Day 1:</strong> Arrive via Geoga Bridge → Maemiseong at late afternoon → stay in Jangmok, Okpo, or Gohyeon.<br />
      <strong>Day 2:</strong> Geoje POW Camp Historic Park in Gohyeon (about 40 minutes by car from Maemiseong) → lunch nearby → if time allows, Windy Hill and Sinseondae on the southern tip (allow roughly an hour from Gohyeon).
    </p>
    <p>
      Maemiseong sits at Geoje’s northeastern tip. Do not plan southern-coast icons as “20 minutes away.”
    </p>

    <h2>Visitor manners</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>This is still a private labor of love opened free to the public. Take your trash with you.</li>
      <li>Some sections have low railings. Keep children close.</li>
      <li>If you bring a pet, use a leash and follow on-site guidance.</li>
    </ul>

